# Conventional Commits

Every commit subject must follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/):

```
<type>(optional-scope): <description>
```

Allowed types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, `revert`.

Rules:

- Subject in imperative mood, lowercase description, no trailing period
- Keep the subject ≤ 72 characters
- Group a change and everything that follows from it (code, tests, docs) into one commit
- Always include a commit body that explains **why** this was changed (motivation, constraint, or failure mode) — never restate the subject or list what the diff already shows
- Breaking changes: `BREAKING CHANGE:` in the body, or `!` after the type/scope

```text
# ❌ BAD
Update vault values
Fixed stuff.

feat(pki): stage remote intermediate issuance for spoke clusters

Stage remote intermediate issuance for spoke clusters.

# ✅ GOOD
feat(pki): stage remote intermediate issuance for spoke clusters

Spokes cannot mint workload certs until an intermediate is
issued from management and staged for local signing.

fix(istio): pin CSR signer address to the rendered service name

The previous default resolved against the management trust domain and
broke spoke enrollment.
```

When creating commits for the user, always use this format (including the HEREDOC flow from the commit workflow).
